narration_manifest: array len 17

--- DESCRIPTION ---
The six circuit parameters, their SI units, and how to convert between multiples and submultiples.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Are Circuit Parameters?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Every electrical circuit can be described using a set of measurable quantities called <dfn class="term" data-def="The measurable electrical quantities that describe the behaviour of a circuit, each with a defined SI or SI-derived unit.">circuit parameters</dfn>. Six parameters are central to this course: potential difference, current, resistance, capacitance, power, and frequency. Understanding what each one means — and being precise about its unit — is essential for every calculation and every exam question that follows. Understanding these parameters and their correct SI units is the foundation for every circuit calculation, because mixing up units such as milliamps and amps, or kilohms and ohms, is one of the most common causes of wrong answers in the exam.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">The Six Parameters and Their Units</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4"><dfn class="term" data-def="The energy transferred per unit charge between two points in a circuit; measured in volts (V).">Potential difference</dfn> (often called voltage) is the electrical "push" that drives current around a circuit. It is the energy transferred per unit of charge, measured in <strong>volts (V)</strong>. Current is the rate of flow of electric charge through a conductor; its SI unit is the <strong>ampere (A)</strong>, commonly shortened to amp. <dfn class="term" data-def="The opposition a component or material offers to the flow of electric current; measured in ohms (Ω).">Resistance</dfn> opposes current flow and is measured in <strong>ohms (Ω)</strong>. Capacitance is the ability of a component to store electric charge; it is measured in <strong>farads (F)</strong>. Power is the rate at which energy is transferred or converted in a circuit, measured in <strong>watts (W)</strong>. Finally, frequency describes how many complete cycles of an alternating signal occur per second, measured in <strong>hertz (Hz)</strong>.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n5" data-revision-tip="Cover this box and write the unit symbol for each parameter from memory: potential difference, current, resistance, capacitance, power, frequency. Check spelling and symbol case — V, A, Ω, F, W, Hz.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The six circuit parameters and their units are: potential difference — volt (V); current — amp (A); resistance — ohm (Ω); capacitance — farad (F); power — watt (W); frequency — hertz (Hz). These are the units you need to know.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n6">Unit Multiples and Submultiples</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Real circuits rarely use base units alone. A radio signal might have a frequency of 100 000 000 Hz — far easier to write as 100 MHz (megahertz). A capacitor might store 0.000 001 F, which is written as 1 µF (microfarad). You need to work with six prefixes: <strong>mega (M)</strong> = 10⁶ (one million), <strong>kilo (k)</strong> = 10³ (one thousand), <strong>milli (m)</strong> = 10⁻³ (one thousandth), <strong>micro (µ)</strong> = 10⁻⁶ (one millionth), <strong>nano (n)</strong> = 10⁻⁹ (one billionth), and <strong>pico (p)</strong> = 10⁻¹² (one trillionth). These prefixes apply to any unit — so kilohms (kΩ), milliamps (mA), and nanofarads (nF) are all fair game in the exam.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">Converting Between Multiples and Submultiples</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">To convert from a prefixed unit to the base unit, multiply by the prefix value. For example, 4.7 kΩ = 4.7 × 1000 = 4700 Ω; 330 µF = 330 × 0.000 001 = 0.000 330 F. To go the other way — from base unit to a prefix — divide. For example, 0.025 A = 25 × 10⁻³ A = 25 mA. A reliable method is to count the steps on a prefix ladder: moving from milli to kilo is six orders of magnitude, so divide by 10⁶. Practise converting in both directions because exam questions will give values in mixed prefixes and expect consistent working.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n10" data-revision-tip="Write the six prefix names and their multiplier values in order from largest (M) to smallest (p). Then practise: convert 2.2 kΩ to Ω, and 470 µF to F, without looking at your notes.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The six prefixes in order from largest to smallest are: mega (M, ×10⁶), kilo (k, ×10³), milli (m, ×10⁻³), micro (µ, ×10⁻⁶), nano (n, ×10⁻⁹), pico (p, ×10⁻¹²). To convert to the base unit, multiply by the prefix value; to convert from the base unit, divide.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Why does the spec distinguish potential difference from voltage?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n11">The term "potential difference" is used rather than just "voltage" because it is technically more precise. Potential difference refers to the difference in electrical potential energy per unit charge between two specific points — it is always measured between two points, never at a single point. "Voltage" is the everyday shorthand, and both terms appear in exam questions. In circuit calculations they are treated identically; the key thing is to use the unit volt (V) and the symbol V in all working.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common mistake: confusing milli (m) with mega (M)</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n12">One of the most common errors in exam papers is mixing up lower-case m (milli, 10⁻³) with upper-case M (mega, 10⁶). They differ by a factor of one billion. Writing 1 mW (milliwatt, one thousandth of a watt) when you mean 1 MW (megawatt, one million watts) would be a catastrophic error in an engineering context — and will lose marks in the exam. Always check the case of the prefix symbol in your answer.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n14">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n15">The six circuit parameters are potential difference (V), current (A), resistance (Ω), capacitance (F), power (W), and frequency (Hz) — learn the unit symbol for each as an inseparable pair.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n16">Six prefixes scale units up or down: mega (M), kilo (k), milli (m), micro (µ), nano (n), pico (p) — to convert to the base unit multiply by the prefix value; to go back, divide.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n17">Case matters: lower-case m is milli (10⁻³) and upper-case M is mega (10⁶) — confusing them in an exam answer will cost marks.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n13">In the exam, unit questions often carry easy marks that students throw away by writing the wrong symbol. Always write the symbol exactly: Ω not ohms, Hz not hz, µ not u for micro. If a question gives a value in kΩ and expects an answer in Ω, show the conversion step explicitly in your working — examiners award method marks even if your final answer is wrong. For prefix questions, learn the ladder (M, k, — , m, µ, n, p) so you can count steps without memorising every power of ten.</p>

