=== L2 Circuit Calculations & Behaviour ===
id: 5566fe26-6855-4a78-8b95-7a25dc1d99c9
slug: lesson-02
status: live tier:both updated:2026-09-02T18:41:29.754866+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 22

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Series and parallel circuits, Ohm's law (V = IR), Watt's law (P = IV), AC vs DC, and signal waveforms.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Electron Flow in Circuits</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Electric current in a conductor is caused by the movement of <dfn class="term" data-def="Negatively charged subatomic particles that carry electric current by drifting through a conductor from the negative terminal to the positive terminal.">electrons</dfn>. In a metal wire, free electrons drift from the negative terminal of the supply towards the positive terminal — this is called electron flow. Conventional current, used in circuit diagrams and calculations, is defined as flowing from positive to negative, which is the opposite direction. For all OCR calculations you use conventional current direction, but you should be aware that actual electron flow goes the other way.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Series and Parallel Circuits</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">In a <dfn class="term" data-def="A circuit in which all components are connected end-to-end in a single loop, so the same current flows through every component.">series circuit</dfn>, components are connected in a single loop. The same current flows through every component, but the supply voltage is shared between them. The total resistance of a series circuit is simply the sum of all individual resistances: R_total = R1 + R2 + R3. If three resistors of 10 Ω, 20 Ω, and 30 Ω are connected in series, the total resistance is 60 Ω.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">In a <dfn class="term" data-def="A circuit in which components are connected across the same two nodes, so each component has the same voltage across it but draws its own current.">parallel circuit</dfn>, components are connected across the same two nodes. Every component receives the full supply voltage, and the total current is the sum of the currents through each branch. The total resistance of a parallel circuit is lower than any individual resistor. For two resistors: 1/R_total = 1/R1 + 1/R2. For three resistors: 1/R_total = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3. Always find 1/R_total first, then take the reciprocal to get R_total.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Without looking, write the formula for total resistance in a series circuit and the formula for total resistance in a parallel circuit. Then apply each to three resistors: 10 Ω, 20 Ω, 30 Ω and check your answers.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Series: R_total = R1 + R2 + R3 (resistances add directly). Parallel (up to three resistors): 1/R_total = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3 — always take the reciprocal of your final sum to get R_total. The OCR specification covers a maximum of three resistors in either configuration.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Ohm's Law: V = I × R</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Ohm's law states that the potential difference across a component is directly proportional to the current through it, provided temperature is constant. The formula is V = I × R, where V is voltage in volts (V), I is current in amps (A), and R is resistance in ohms (Ω). You must be able to rearrange this formula: I = V ÷ R to find current; R = V ÷ I to find resistance. For example, if a resistor has 12 V across it and a current of 0.5 A flows through it, its resistance is R = 12 ÷ 0.5 = 24 Ω.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Watt's Law: P = I × V</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10">The power dissipated by a component equals the current through it multiplied by the voltage across it: P = I × V, where P is power in watts (W). This formula can be rearranged: I = P ÷ V; V = P ÷ I. For example, a motor operating at 6 V and drawing a current of 2 A has a power consumption of P = 2 × 6 = 12 W. P = I × V is the formula most directly tested; the derived forms P = I²R and P = V²/R can be used when voltage is not directly given — check your specification formula sheet to confirm which forms are supplied in the exam.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n11" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write both formulas — V = I × R and P = I × V — from memory, including the units for each symbol. Then rearrange each formula three ways to practise isolating every variable.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Ohm's law: V = I × R (V in volts, I in amps, R in ohms). Watt's law: P = I × V (P in watts). Both formulas must be used with base units — convert any kΩ or mA to Ω or A before substituting. Remove 'OCR does not require P = I² × R.' Teach P = IV as primary; note that derived forms are also standard.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">AC and DC: Types of Current</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">Direct current (DC) flows in one direction only; its voltage remains constant over time. Batteries supply DC. Alternating current (AC) repeatedly reverses direction; the voltage rises and falls in a regular cycle. The UK mains supply is AC at 230 V, 50 Hz. Most electronic circuits use DC internally, but mains power is AC — a transformer and rectifier convert AC to DC in a mains adaptor.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">Analogue and Digital Signals</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">An analogue signal varies continuously and can take any value within a range — like the output of a microphone or a temperature sensor. A digital signal takes only two discrete states: high (1) or low (0). The waveforms for these signals look very different: an analogue signal produces a smooth sine wave with a continuous range of amplitudes, while a digital signal produces a square wave that switches sharply between two voltage levels. Key waveform properties include: amplitude (the peak height of the wave), frequency (the number of complete cycles per second in Hz), and periodic time (the duration of one complete cycle, T = 1 ÷ f).</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Worked example: parallel resistance calculation</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n16">Three resistors — 10 Ω, 20 Ω, and 40 Ω — are connected in parallel. Find the total resistance.

Step 1: 1/R_total = 1/10 + 1/20 + 1/40
Step 2: Convert to a common denominator of 40: 4/40 + 2/40 + 1/40 = 7/40
Step 3: R_total = 40 ÷ 7 ≈ 5.71 Ω

Notice that the total resistance (5.71 Ω) is less than the smallest individual resistor (10 Ω) — this is always true in a parallel circuit. Show all three steps in exam working to maximise method marks.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Advantages and disadvantages of analogue vs digital signals</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n17">Analogue signals capture the full continuous range of a measurement, which can mean higher fidelity — a microphone output faithfully follows every nuance of sound. However, analogue signals are susceptible to noise: any interference adds to the signal and cannot easily be removed. Digital signals can be regenerated perfectly from their two-state nature — if a signal is noisy but clearly above the threshold, a circuit can restore it to a clean 1. Digital signals are also easier to process, store, and transmit reliably, which is why almost all modern communication and computing systems use digital formats. The trade-off is that converting from analogue to digital (via an ADC) introduces a small quantisation error — the continuous signal is approximated in discrete steps.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n19">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">In a series circuit R_total = R1 + R2 + R3; in a parallel circuit 1/R_total = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3 — always take the reciprocal to get R_total, and always check it is less than the smallest individual resistor.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">Ohm's law (V = I × R) and Watt's law (P = I × V) must be rearranged to find any variable — convert all values to base units first and show formula, substitution, and answer with unit in exam working.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">AC reverses direction cyclically (mains = 230 V, 50 Hz); DC flows in one direction (batteries). Digital signals are two-state and noise-resistant; analogue signals are continuous but susceptible to interference.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n18">For Ohm's law and power calculations, always convert units to base units before substituting: mA to A (÷1000), kΩ to Ω (×1000). Show your working in three clear steps — formula, substitution, answer with unit — because OCR awards method marks even if the final value is wrong. For parallel resistance, a very common error is forgetting to take the reciprocal at the end: after finding 1/R_total, divide 1 by that value to get R_total in Ω.</p>

