=== L3 Representing Electronic Systems ===
id: dc861baa-c259-417a-a65e-00cfb9a19446
slug: lesson-03
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:53:46.481078+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 18

--- DESCRIPTION ---
System block diagrams, open and closed loop systems, circuit schematics, and PCB layouts explained.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why We Use Different Representations</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Electronic systems can be represented in three different ways: <dfn class="term" data-def="A simplified diagram that shows a system as a series of labelled rectangles connected by arrows, without showing individual components.">system block diagrams</dfn>, circuit schematics, and PCB layouts. Each method has a different purpose and level of detail. A block diagram gives an overview of what a system does; a schematic shows exactly which components are used and how they connect; a PCB layout shows where components are physically placed on a board. The OCR specification requires you to interpret — and partially complete — all three types, for systems with up to four blocks.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">System Block Diagrams and the Systems Approach</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">The systems approach breaks any electronic system into three fundamental stages: <strong>input</strong>, <strong>process</strong>, and <strong>output</strong>. In a block diagram, each stage is drawn as a labelled rectangle, and arrows show the direction of signal flow between blocks. A simple torch system might show: battery (input) → switch (process) → LED (output). Block diagrams are quick to draw and easy to understand, making them ideal for planning and communicating a design before any components are chosen.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">A key distinction the specification requires is between <dfn class="term" data-def="A system in which there is no feedback path — the output has no influence on the input or the processing stage.">open loop systems</dfn> and <dfn class="term" data-def="A system in which part of the output is fed back to the input to adjust and regulate the system's behaviour.">closed loop systems</dfn>. In an open loop system, information flows only in one direction from input to output; there is no feedback. A microwave oven set to run for two minutes regardless of food temperature is open loop — the output (heated food) does not influence the input. In a closed loop system, a feedback loop carries information from the output back to the input. A central heating thermostat is closed loop: the room temperature (output) is fed back and compared with the set temperature (input) to switch the boiler on or off.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover this and sketch a closed loop block diagram from memory, including the input, process, output, and feedback arrow. Label each block and use signal arrows to show direction of information flow.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Open loop: input → process → output (no feedback). Closed loop: input → process → output, plus a feedback arrow from output back to input. Signal arrows in block diagrams must always show the direction of signal flow. The OCR specification tests you on interpreting and completing block diagrams with up to four blocks.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Circuit Schematics</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A standardised diagram that uses agreed circuit symbols to show how components are connected in an electronic circuit.">circuit schematic</dfn> (also called a circuit diagram) uses standardised symbols to represent components and shows how they are electrically connected. Every component has a specific symbol — a resistor is shown as a rectangle (BS standard) or a zigzag (US standard); an LED as a diode symbol with two outward arrows; a battery as long and short alternating lines. Schematics do not show the physical size or location of components — they focus purely on the electrical connections. When interpreting a schematic, trace each connection to identify the circuit topology (series or parallel) and the function of each component.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">PCB Layouts</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10">A printed circuit board (PCB) layout shows the actual physical arrangement of components and the copper tracks that connect them on the board. Unlike a schematic, a PCB layout is drawn to scale and shows where each component is soldered. PCB design software produces both the schematic and the corresponding PCB layout; the layout is then used to manufacture the physical board. When reading a PCB layout, components are identified by reference designators (R1 for first resistor, C1 for first capacitor, etc.), and copper tracks appear as solid lines or polygons connecting pads.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n11" data-revision-tip="Write from memory: what does a schematic show that a block diagram does not? What does a PCB layout show that a schematic does not? This comparison question appears regularly in OCR exams.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Block diagram: shows system function and signal flow, no component detail. Circuit schematic: shows component types and electrical connections using standard symbols. PCB layout: shows physical size, position, and copper track routing of every component on the board. Each representation serves a different stage of the engineering design process.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How to complete a partially drawn block diagram in an exam</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n12">When an OCR exam question asks you to complete a block diagram, follow these steps: (1) Identify which blocks are already drawn and what stage they represent (input, process, output, or feedback). (2) Identify what is missing — a block label, a signal arrow, or a feedback path. (3) For each empty box, ask: is it converting an environmental signal into an electrical signal? If so, it is an input/sensor. Is it controlling or switching another component? That is a process block. Is it converting electrical energy into light, sound or movement? That is an output. (4) Draw signal arrows between every block to show the direction of signal flow — arrows on the feedback path must point from output back towards input. Always use a ruler and label every block clearly.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Real-world example: temperature alarm system</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n13">A freezer temperature alarm is a classic closed loop example for the exam. Input: NTC thermistor senses temperature. Process: microcontroller compares measured temperature with a threshold. Output: buzzer sounds if temperature is too high. Feedback: the microcontroller continuously reads the thermistor output, so the system self-regulates — it checks and re-checks the temperature rather than acting once and stopping. This feedback loop makes the system closed loop. In the block diagram, draw the feedback arrow from the output stage back to the process block, with an arrow pointing towards the process block.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n15">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n16">Three representation methods serve different purposes: block diagrams show system function and signal flow; schematics show component connections using standard symbols; PCB layouts show physical size and copper track routing.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n17">Open loop systems have no feedback path — input, process, output only. Closed loop systems include a feedback arrow from output back to input, allowing the system to self-regulate.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">To complete a partially drawn block diagram, identify each block's stage (input, process, output, or feedback), add missing labels and signal arrows in the correct direction, and check the arrow on any feedback path points back towards the process block.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n14">Questions on block diagrams often ask you to state whether a system is open or closed loop and justify your answer — you must refer to the presence or absence of a feedback path, not just say 'there is/isn't feedback'. For schematic questions, use the standard BS circuit symbol names (e.g. 'NPN transistor', 'LED', 'thermistor') — vague descriptions like 'a triangle shape' will not gain marks. When completing PCB or schematic questions, check that every component connects to both the positive and the negative supply line, or the circuit will not be complete.</p>

