=== L4 Electronic Components & Devices ===
id: 60e93465-c7f6-4aa4-ac0d-b9aa3bcbbd6f
slug: lesson-04
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:34:46.902237+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 18

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Input devices, output devices, process components, passive components, power supplies, and wiring types.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Input Components and Devices</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Input devices convert a physical quantity or user action into an electrical signal. The OCR specification covers several switch types: the <dfn class="term" data-def="A switch with a single input and a single output contact that either makes or breaks the circuit — the most basic on/off switch type.">Single Pole Single Throw (SPST) switch</dfn> simply opens or closes one circuit; a <strong>push-to-make switch</strong> connects the circuit only while pressed; a <strong>push-to-break switch</strong> disconnects the circuit while pressed. A <strong>tilt switch</strong> contains a small ball bearing that completes or breaks the circuit when tilted — used in safety cut-offs. A <strong>reed switch</strong> is a magnetically operated switch: two metal contacts inside a glass capsule close when a magnet is brought close — common in door and window alarms. A <strong>Quantum Tunnelling Composite (QTC) switch</strong> uses a material whose resistance changes when compressed, enabling pressure-sensitive switching without moving parts.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">Sensor input devices include: a <dfn class="term" data-def="A resistor whose resistance decreases as light intensity increases; used to detect ambient light levels.">Light Dependent Resistor (LDR)</dfn>, whose resistance falls as light increases — used in automatic lighting circuits; a <strong>photodiode</strong>, which conducts in proportion to incident light — faster and more precise than an LDR; an <strong>NTC thermistor</strong> (Negative Temperature Coefficient), whose resistance falls as temperature rises — used in temperature measurement and control; a <strong>pressure sensor</strong>, which converts mechanical force into an electrical signal; an <strong>infrared sensor</strong>, which detects infrared radiation — used in motion detectors and remote controls; and <strong>smart (WiFi-enabled) sensors</strong>, which connect to a network to transmit data remotely. <strong>Touch screens</strong> are also listed as input devices — they detect the position of a finger or stylus on a display surface.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Process Components and Devices</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5">Process components manipulate signals between input and output. <strong>Amplifiers</strong> increase signal voltage or current. <strong>Counters</strong> count pulses or events. <strong>Timers</strong> generate a timed output after a set delay (e.g. the 555 timer IC). <strong>Latches</strong> store a binary state — once triggered, they hold their output until reset. <strong>Pulse generators</strong> produce repetitive clock signals. <strong>Analogue to digital converters (ADCs)</strong> sample an analogue input and convert it to a binary number — essential for connecting sensors to microcontrollers. Logic functions (AND, OR, NOT, NAND) are digital process components whose outputs depend on combinations of binary inputs. You must know their truth tables and be able to apply them singly and in two-level combinations.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover this and list from memory: three switch types, three sensor types, and three output device types. This is a common 'name and describe' question style in OCR exams.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Input switches: SPST, push-to-make, push-to-break, tilt, reed, QTC. Input sensors: LDR, photodiode, NTC thermistor, pressure sensor, infrared sensor, smart sensor. Output devices: lamps, LEDs, LED displays, LCDs, buzzers, piezo sounders, motors. Drivers/interface: NPN transistor, Darlington driver, relay. Passive: resistors (fixed, variable), diodes, capacitors (polarised, non-polarised).</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Output Components and Devices</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8"><strong>Lamps</strong> produce light from current; they require relatively high current and are less efficient than LEDs. <strong>Light Emitting Diodes (LEDs)</strong> are semiconductor devices that emit light when forward biased — low current, long life, available in many colours. <strong>LED displays</strong> (7-segment and dot-matrix) are arrays of LEDs configured to display numbers, letters, and symbols. <strong>Liquid Crystal Displays (LCDs)</strong> use liquid crystal cells to modulate light — very low power, used in clocks, meters, and screens. <strong>Buzzers</strong> use an electromagnetic mechanism to produce sound when current flows; <strong>piezo sounders</strong> use a piezoelectric disc that vibrates when an alternating voltage is applied — smaller and more power-efficient than buzzers. <strong>Motors</strong> convert electrical energy into rotational mechanical energy.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Drivers, Passive Components, Power Supplies and Wiring</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10">Higher-power output devices need <strong>driver</strong> components because microcontrollers supply only a few milliamps. An <strong>NPN transistor</strong> amplifies current — a small base current switches a much larger collector-emitter current. A <dfn class="term" data-def="A pair of NPN transistors wired so that the output of the first drives the base of the second, giving a very high combined current gain, used to drive high-power loads such as motors.">Darlington driver</dfn> pairs two NPN transistors for very high current gain. A <strong>relay</strong> closes a separate high-power switching contact using a small electromagnet current, electrically isolating the control circuit from the load. Passive components need no supply: <strong>fixed resistors</strong> set resistance; <strong>variable resistors</strong> adjust it; <strong>diodes</strong> pass current in one direction only; <strong>capacitors</strong> store charge — polarised (electrolytic) types must be connected the right way round, non-polarised types can go either way. Power supply options: <strong>batteries</strong> (portable DC); <strong>photovoltaic cells</strong> (renewable, light-dependent output); <strong>supercapacitors</strong> (rapid charge/discharge, limited energy storage); <strong>mains adaptors</strong> (stable DC from AC mains). Wiring: <strong>single-strand (solid core)</strong> holds shape — ideal for breadboard; <strong>multi-strand (flexible)</strong> bends without breaking — used where connections must flex.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n11" data-revision-tip="For each power supply type, write one advantage and one disadvantage from memory. Common exam question: 'Suggest a suitable power supply for a remote sensor in a field and justify your choice.'">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>NPN transistors and Darlington drivers switch high-current loads using a small control current. Relays provide electrical isolation between the control circuit and the load — essential when switching mains-voltage devices from a low-voltage microcontroller. Polarised capacitors must be connected with the correct polarity or they can be damaged.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How does an NTC thermistor work and why does resistance fall with temperature?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n12">NTC stands for Negative Temperature Coefficient. As temperature rises, more electrons gain enough energy to become charge carriers, so more current flows for the same voltage — meaning resistance is lower (R = V ÷ I). This resistance change is used in a voltage divider circuit to produce a varying voltage that a microcontroller reads and compares with a threshold to trigger a heating or cooling output.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Choosing the right power supply for a given application</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n13">Battery: portable but must be replaced or recharged — good for handheld devices, poor for long-term field sensors. Photovoltaic cell: renewable and maintenance-free in daylight, but output varies with light and a buffer store is needed for night use. Supercapacitor: charges and discharges very rapidly, long cycle life, but stores far less total energy than a battery — suited to brief backup or energy harvesting. Mains adaptor: reliable, constant output, but needs a socket — ideal for fixed, indoor devices only.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n15">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n16">Input devices convert physical quantities into electrical signals: switches respond to user action; sensors (LDR, NTC thermistor, photodiode, infrared, pressure, smart) respond to environmental quantities.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n17">Output devices convert electrical signals: LEDs and lamps produce light; buzzers and piezo sounders produce sound; motors produce rotational movement; LCDs and LED displays produce visual information.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">Driver components (NPN transistors, Darlington drivers, relays) allow a low-current control signal to switch a high-current load; relays also provide electrical isolation between the control circuit and the switched circuit.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n14">When a question asks you to 'identify a suitable input device' or 'choose an appropriate output component', always justify your choice in terms of the physical quantity being sensed or the form of energy being converted — do not just name the component. For example: 'an LDR is suitable because its resistance changes with light intensity, which can be used to vary the voltage in a circuit to detect day and night.' Component symbol questions require you to recognise the symbol, not just name the component — practise sketching the symbols for LED, LDR, NPN transistor, relay, and NTC thermistor.</p>

