--- DESCRIPTION ---
Microcontrollers, PLCs, and the three types of programming language used in programmable electronic systems.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Makes a System Programmable?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">A <dfn class="term" data-def="An electronic system whose behaviour is determined by a stored program that can be changed without altering the hardware.">programmable system</dfn> differs from a fixed (hard-wired) circuit because its behaviour is controlled by software — a set of instructions stored in memory — rather than by the physical arrangement of components alone. In a hard-wired circuit, changing what the circuit does means physically re-wiring it. In a programmable system, the same hardware can be given an entirely new function by rewriting the program. This flexibility is one of the greatest advantages of programmable systems in engineering design.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Microcontrollers</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A single integrated circuit that contains a processor, memory, and programmable input/output peripherals, used to control electronic systems.">microcontroller</dfn> is a compact integrated circuit containing a processor (CPU), program memory (flash), data memory (RAM), and configurable input/output (I/O) pins — all on a single chip. It reads inputs from sensors and switches, executes a stored program, and produces outputs to control actuators and displays. Well-known microcontroller platforms include the Arduino (using the ATmega328P) and the BBC micro:bit. Microcontrollers are ideal for low-power, low-cost, embedded applications where a full computer would be excessive — for example, a smart thermostat, a traffic light controller, or an automatic plant watering system.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">The advantages of using a microcontroller include: flexibility (the program can be changed without hardware alteration); reduced component count (one chip replaces many logic ICs); low cost for mass production; and reliability (no moving parts). The disadvantages include: the need for programming skills to write or modify the code; dependence on a power supply that does not fail; and potential vulnerability to software bugs that can cause unpredictable behaviour.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Write from memory: three advantages and two disadvantages of using a microcontroller compared to a hard-wired circuit. This evaluation question is one of the most common formats in the exam.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Advantages of a programmable system over hard-wired: easily reprogrammed, reduced component count, flexible for multiple applications, easier to update or correct. Disadvantages: requires programming knowledge, relies on power supply stability, software bugs can cause faults, initial setup is more complex than simple hard-wired circuits.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Programmable Logic Controllers (PLCs)</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A ruggedised industrial computer designed to control manufacturing processes, machinery, and production lines in real-time, programmed using ladder logic or similar languages.">Programmable Logic Controller (PLC)</dfn> is a specialised industrial microcontroller built for harsh environments — it is designed to be shock-resistant, tolerant of temperature extremes and electromagnetic interference, and capable of controlling multiple high-power outputs simultaneously. PLCs are programmed using ladder logic (a graphical language resembling relay circuit diagrams) or structured text, and are used extensively in factories, water treatment plants, and automated assembly lines. Compared to a general-purpose microcontroller, a PLC is more expensive, but far more robust and better suited to safety-critical industrial environments where reliability is paramount.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Types of Programming Language and Systems</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10">There are three types of programming language or system to know. A <strong>text-based language</strong> (such as Python, C, or C++) uses written code consisting of commands, variables, and syntax rules. It is the most powerful and flexible option, offering full control over system behaviour, but requires the programmer to learn the language syntax and is harder for beginners to read. A <strong>block-based editor</strong> (such as Scratch, MakeCode, or MIT App Inventor) represents program instructions as visual blocks that snap together, eliminating syntax errors and making logic easy to see — ideal for beginners and for teaching purposes, but less efficient for complex programs. A <strong>flowchart system</strong> (such as Flowol or similar graphical tools) uses standard flowchart symbols (start/stop ovals, process rectangles, decision diamonds) connected by arrows to represent program logic — visual and easy to plan, but can become cluttered for large programs and is not used in professional development.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n11" data-revision-tip="Cover this box and write one advantage and one disadvantage for each of the three programming types: text-based, block-based, and flowchart. This comparison appears frequently in 4-mark and 6-mark OCR questions.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Text-based: most flexible, full control, but requires syntax knowledge and harder for beginners. Block-based: no syntax errors, visually clear, easy for beginners, but less efficient for complex tasks. Flowchart: easy to plan logic visually, but cluttered for large programs and not used professionally. You are not required to write code in any specific language.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How does a microcontroller differ from a PLC in practice?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n12">A microcontroller (e.g. Arduino, micro:bit) is a low-cost, compact chip typically used in prototyping, educational projects, and consumer electronics. It is programmed via a USB connection from a laptop and runs on 3.3 V or 5 V. A PLC is a larger, more expensive, industrial-grade unit designed for continuous operation in electrically noisy factory environments — it operates from 24 V DC or 110/240 V AC, has screw-terminal I/O connections for industrial wiring, and is built to safety standards that a microcontroller does not meet. For a school project controlling LEDs and a buzzer, a microcontroller is entirely suitable. For controlling a bottling plant's conveyor and filling machines that must run 24 hours a day without failure, a PLC is the appropriate choice.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Choosing the right programming language for an application</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n13">When an OCR exam question asks you to recommend a programming language type, consider the user's experience and the complexity of the task. A Year 7 student creating a simple flashing LED sequence: block-based editor — no syntax to learn, immediate visual feedback, drag-and-drop makes it accessible. A university engineer writing firmware for a commercial product: text-based language (C or Python) — full control over memory and timing, code can be optimised, and it integrates with professional toolchains. A teacher planning the logic of a new control system before implementation: flowchart system — easy to map out decision trees and loops before writing any code, acts as a design document. You need to compare the relative merits for a given application — always state why the chosen type suits the context, not just what its general advantages are.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n15">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n16">A programmable system stores its behaviour as software, allowing the same hardware to be reprogrammed for different tasks — unlike a hard-wired circuit which must be physically altered to change its function.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n17">Microcontrollers (compact, low-cost, flexible) suit prototyping and consumer electronics; PLCs (rugged, industrial-grade, expensive) suit continuous factory and safety-critical environments.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">Three programming types: text-based (powerful, requires syntax knowledge), block-based (visual, beginner-friendly, no syntax errors), and flowchart (good for planning logic, not used professionally) — choose based on the user's experience and the complexity of the task.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n14">When evaluating microcontrollers versus hard-wired circuits, the most creditworthy points are specific: say 'the microcontroller can be reprogrammed without changing the hardware, so if the client changes the specification the engineer only needs to upload new code' rather than 'it is more flexible'. For programming language questions, always relate your answer to the specific application in the question — a beginner student and a professional engineer need different tools, and you must explain why. Writing actual programs is not assessed, so you will never be asked to produce code.</p>

