=== L7 Programming & Flowcharts ===
id: 67d29144-93cb-4715-b225-49fc2cca2aef
slug: lesson-07
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:35:48.666166+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 19

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Types of programming languages for programmable systems — text-based, block-based, and flowchart — and how.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Programming a Programmable System</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A component such as a microcontroller or PLC that can be programmed to control a system's behaviour by executing a sequence of instructions.">programmable component</dfn> such as a microcontroller does nothing useful until it is given a program — a precise sequence of instructions that tells it how to respond to inputs and control outputs. The specification identifies three types of programming language and system used to write or represent those instructions: text-based languages, block-based editors, and flowchart systems. Each has different characteristics, and the right choice depends on the complexity of the task and the experience of the programmer.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Text-Based Programming Languages</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A programming language where instructions are written as lines of typed code using a specific syntax, such as C, Python or Arduino's language.">text-based language</dfn> requires the programmer to type instructions using the exact keywords and syntax of the chosen language — for example, C, MicroPython, or the Arduino language. Text-based code gives the greatest flexibility and control: any logic, however complex, can be expressed, and the resulting programs run efficiently on a microcontroller's processor. The disadvantage is the learning curve — syntax errors cause the program to fail, and beginners may find it hard to understand code written by others. Text-based code is the standard approach in professional and industrial engineering.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n5" data-revision-tip="Cover this and list all three programming types, plus one advantage and one disadvantage of each, entirely from memory.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The three programming types are: text-based (written code, most powerful, steepest learning curve), block-based (drag-and-drop visual blocks, easier for beginners, limited for complex programs) and flowchart systems (visual symbols representing the flow of control, easy to plan and communicate, but can become complex to follow on large programs).</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n6">Block-Based Editors</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Block-based editors such as Scratch or Microsoft MakeCode represent program instructions as graphical blocks that are dragged and snapped together on screen. Each block has a fixed shape that only allows valid connections, so syntax errors are almost impossible. This makes block-based editors highly accessible for beginners and for quickly prototyping simple control logic. The limitation is that complex logic quickly becomes cluttered and hard to follow, and not all programming concepts — particularly low-level hardware control — can be represented easily with pre-defined blocks. Block-based editors are widely used in schools and for rapid concept testing.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Comparing text-based and block-based approaches</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8">Text-based code transfers directly to professional and industrial settings — skills learned in Arduino C or Python are used in real engineering roles. Block-based editors, by contrast, are primarily educational tools. Some platforms such as MakeCode for the micro:bit allow the programmer to switch between block view and the equivalent text-based (Python or JavaScript) view of the same program, which helps learners make the transition. For the OCR exam, you need to understand the relative merits of each type and be able to argue which is more suitable for a given scenario — you are not expected to write actual code.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Flowchart Systems</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A diagram that uses standard symbols — ovals, rectangles, diamonds, and parallelograms — to represent the sequence of steps and decisions in a program or process.">flowchart</dfn> uses standardised shapes connected by arrows to represent the flow of control through a program. The key symbols are: an oval for start/end (terminal), a rectangle for a process step (such as 'turn LED on'), a diamond for a decision (a yes/no question), and a parallelogram for input or output. Flowchart software such as Flowol or PicLogicator can directly interpret a drawn flowchart as a program and run it on a connected programmable system. The advantage of flowcharts is that they make the logic of a program immediately visible, making them easy to plan, communicate, and debug. The disadvantage is that large, complex programs produce sprawling flowcharts that become difficult to read and maintain.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n11" data-revision-tip="Sketch the four key flowchart symbols from memory and write their names next to each: terminal (oval), process (rectangle), decision (diamond), input/output (parallelogram).">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The four standard flowchart symbols are: oval — terminal (start/stop); rectangle — process (an action or calculation); diamond — decision (a yes/no question that creates a branch); parallelogram — input or output (e.g. reading a sensor or switching an output device). Arrows show the direction of flow between each step.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">Using a Flowchart to Control a Programmable System</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">A flowchart directly models how a microcontroller or PLC executes a program. Consider a simple temperature alarm: the flowchart starts (oval), reads the temperature sensor (parallelogram — input), then reaches a decision diamond: 'Is temperature above 30°C?' If yes, an arrow leads to a process rectangle 'Turn on buzzer' and an output parallelogram; if no, an arrow loops back to read the sensor again. This loop continues indefinitely. The flowchart both communicates the design to other engineers and, in systems like Flowol, can be executed directly as the program itself. Identifying where decision diamonds create loops or branches is key to both drawing and interpreting flowcharts in the exam.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Which programming method should you choose?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n14">The choice of programming method depends on the experience of the programmer and the complexity of the task. For a beginner or for planning and communicating a simple control algorithm, flowcharts and block-based editors are the most accessible. For a complex, high-performance system that needs precise hardware control or efficient code, a text-based language is most appropriate. In an exam scenario question, always link your choice to the programmer's skill level, the complexity of the program required, and whether the code needs to be maintainable by others in a team.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n16">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n17">The three programming types are text-based (powerful but demanding), block-based (accessible but limited), and flowchart systems (visual, easy to plan and communicate, but unwieldy for large programs).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">The four standard flowchart symbols are oval (terminal), rectangle (process), diamond (decision), and parallelogram (input/output); arrows show the direction of program flow.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">A flowchart can directly represent the control logic of a programmable system such as a microcontroller — decisions create branches and loops that mirror how the hardware responds to inputs.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n15">Flowchart questions may ask you to complete, correct, or draw part of a flowchart. Always use the correct symbol shapes — drawing a process step as a diamond will lose marks. For 'compare' or 'discuss' questions about programming types, structure your answer around named advantages and disadvantages of each type, linked to the specific scenario in the question. A common mistake is describing what a flowchart looks like without explaining why the visual structure makes it easier to plan or debug a program compared with written code.</p>

