=== L9 Commercial PCB Production ===
id: 89ce89f1-fd97-41a6-beff-0b6124d29cda
slug: lesson-09
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:40:27.762566+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 19

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How printed circuit boards are produced commercially — photo etching, CAM milling, and the types of PCB.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">From Prototype to Printed Circuit Board</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Once a circuit design has been validated on a breadboard or stripboard prototype, the next step in commercial production is to manufacture a <dfn class="term" data-def="A board made from insulating material with copper tracks on its surface that permanently connect electronic components in the designed circuit.">printed circuit board (PCB)</dfn>. A PCB replaces the hand-wired connections of a prototype with precise copper tracks that are reproduced identically on every board, reducing errors and enabling mass manufacture. The OCR specification covers two production methods — photo etching and CAM milling/routing — and three types of PCB: single-sided, double-sided, and flexible.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Photo Etching</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4"><dfn class="term" data-def="A PCB production process in which a UV-sensitive coating on a copper-clad board is exposed through a mask to harden the desired track pattern, then the unwanted copper is dissolved away in an etchant chemical.">Photo etching</dfn> (also called the photochemical process or UV etching) uses ultraviolet light and a chemical etchant to transfer a track pattern onto a copper-clad board. The process has several distinct stages. First, the PCB layout is printed as a positive or negative image onto a transparent film (the photomask). Second, the copper-clad board — coated with a UV-sensitive layer called photoresist — is placed under the photomask and exposed to UV light. Where the UV light reaches the photoresist it hardens (or softens, depending on whether positive or negative resist is used). Third, a developer chemical removes the unexposed (soft) photoresist, leaving the hardened photoresist protecting only the intended copper tracks. Fourth, the board is immersed in an etchant solution — typically ferric chloride or ammonium persulphate — which dissolves all exposed copper, leaving only the copper beneath the protective resist. Fifth, the remaining photoresist is stripped away to reveal the finished copper tracks. Finally, holes are drilled for through-hole components. Photo etching produces very fine, accurate tracks and is the dominant method in commercial PCB production.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n5" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write the five stages of photo etching in order from memory: photomask, UV exposure, development, etching, resist removal.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The five stages of photo etching are: (1) print photomask; (2) UV exposure — UV light hardens the photoresist over the track pattern; (3) develop — remove unexposed resist; (4) etch — dissolve unprotected copper in etchant chemical; (5) strip remaining resist to reveal copper tracks. Etchant chemicals are hazardous — proper safety precautions (gloves, goggles, neutralisation and disposal) are always required.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n6">CAM Milling/Routing</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n7"><dfn class="term" data-def="A PCB production method in which a computer-controlled milling machine physically cuts channels through the copper layer of a board to isolate and define the copper tracks.">CAM milling</dfn> (Computer-Aided Manufacture milling or routing) uses a CNC (computer numerically controlled) machine fitted with a small cutting bit to physically remove copper from a copper-clad board. The PCB design file is sent directly to the milling machine, which follows the track outlines precisely, cutting away the copper between tracks so that only the desired conductive paths remain. The advantages of CAM milling are that no chemicals are required (making it safer and more environmentally friendly), it produces a board very quickly from a design file, and it is well-suited to school or workshop use for one-off prototype PCBs. The disadvantages are that the track resolution is coarser than photo etching (minimum track spacing is limited by the cutter size), and the process is slower and less cost-effective for large-volume production than photo etching.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Photo etching vs CAM milling — which is better?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8">Photo etching is favoured for commercial mass production because it can produce very fine tracks (important for densely packed components), is fast for large batches, and the same photomask can be reused to make hundreds of identical boards. CAM milling is favoured for low-quantity, rapid-prototyping situations — particularly in schools and small engineering workshops — because there are no hazardous chemicals involved, no need for a darkroom or UV exposure unit, and the board is ready almost immediately after the CNC program finishes. For an exam question comparing the two, always link your choice to the scale of production and the track resolution required.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Types of PCB: Single-Sided</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A PCB with copper tracks on one side only — the simplest and lowest-cost type, used in straightforward consumer electronics.">single-sided PCB</dfn> has copper tracks on one side of the insulating board only. Components are mounted on the opposite (component) side and their leads pass through holes to be soldered to the tracks underneath. Single-sided boards are the simplest and cheapest to manufacture. They are suitable for circuits that are not very complex — where all the routing can be achieved without tracks needing to cross. Their limitation is that complex circuits cannot be routed on a single side without track crossovers, which are not possible without bridges or additional wiring.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n11" data-revision-tip="Write the three PCB types and one advantage and one limitation of each from memory — single-sided (simple/cheap), double-sided (complex routing), flexible (bends and curves).">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Three PCB types: single-sided (copper one side — cheapest, suits simple circuits); double-sided (copper both sides — suits complex circuits, can use vias to connect layers); flexible (copper tracks on thin flexible substrate — suits wearables and curved enclosures, but more expensive and harder to manufacture).</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">Types of PCB: Double-Sided and Flexible</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">A double-sided PCB has copper tracks on both sides of the board. Connections between the two layers are made through small plated holes called vias. Double-sided boards greatly increase the routing density that can be achieved — complex circuits such as microcontroller development boards and motor controllers are typically double-sided. The manufacturing process is more involved (both sides must be etched and aligned precisely), and cost is higher than single-sided. A flexible PCB uses a thin, flexible polymer substrate rather than the rigid fibreglass (FR4) of standard boards, allowing the circuit to bend, fold, or conform to a curved surface. Flexible PCBs are used in products where space is very restricted or the board must flex during use — for example, inside a fold-up phone, a camera module, or a wearable health monitor. They are more expensive to manufacture than rigid boards and can be damaged by repeated sharp bending.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Choosing the right PCB type for a product</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n14">The choice of PCB type is driven by circuit complexity, product form factor, and budget. If the circuit is simple (such as a basic LED driver or a small sensor module) and cost is a priority, a single-sided PCB is appropriate. If the circuit is complex — many components, dense interconnections — a double-sided PCB allows the routing to be split between layers. If the product must fit inside a curved or flexible enclosure, or the circuit board itself must flex during use (such as a fitness tracker wristband), a flexible PCB is required despite its higher cost. In exam answers, always justify your chosen PCB type with a specific reason linked to the product scenario.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n16">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n17">Photo etching uses UV light and chemical etchant to produce fine copper tracks and is the main commercial method; CAM milling uses a CNC cutter with no chemicals and is better for rapid one-off prototypes.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">Single-sided PCBs have copper on one side (cheap, simple circuits); double-sided have copper on both sides (complex routing via vias); flexible PCBs use a bendable substrate for curved or wearable products.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">Etchant chemicals used in photo etching are hazardous — gloves and goggles must be worn, and waste must be neutralised and disposed of correctly according to safety guidelines.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n15">Questions on PCB production often ask you to compare photo etching and CAM milling — focus on the key distinctions: photo etching uses chemicals (etchant, photoresist) and is suited to fine-track commercial production; CAM milling uses a CNC cutter with no chemicals and is suited to rapid one-off prototypes in a school or workshop. For PCB type questions, the distinguishing features are the number of copper layers (one vs two) and whether the substrate is rigid or flexible — always link these features to a specific advantage or application in your answer.</p>

