+    "expect": "Mastering (5–6 marks): balanced discussion with at least two developed factors. For example: total supply voltage = 1.5 + 1.5 = 3 V; the student must calculate the series resistor needed to limit current to 20 mA — using V = I × R (with the voltage dropped across the resistor = supply voltage minus Change 'LED forward voltage ≈ 2 V if LED Vf ≈ 1 V, R = 2 ÷ 0.020 = 100 Ω' to 'LED forward voltage (Vf) for a typical red LED ≈ 2 V; voltage across resistor = 3 − 2 = 1 V; R = 1 ÷ 0.020 = 50 Ω (accept any value using a realistic Vf of 1.8–2.2 V with correct arithmetic)') — this prevents the LED from burning out (first factor, developed with calculation). Contrasted with: power consumption affects battery life — P = I × V = 0.020 × 3 = 0.06 W means the batteries will drain over time, so the student must consider capacity and efficiency in the design (second factor, developed with calculation and application to the scenario).\nSecure (3–4 marks): two factors identified and briefly explained with some reference to the scenario.\nDeveloping (2 marks): one factor identified with limited explanation.\nEmerging (1 mark): a single relevant point with no development. [6 marks]",
